Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Unraveling the Svalbard Warp
- Story & Context: When boiler three failed inside the permafrost archive, technician Amrit Singh slapped a contact mic onto the main vault door and blew a single, continuous baritone sax low-note for twenty minutes to stop the seeds from freezing. As the heat dipped toward absolute zero, the rest of the night-shift crew grabbed iron pipes and pounded out a frantic, distorted chaal beat on the metal floorboards to keep their limbs from going numb. The resulting recording, captured on an emergency cassette recorder, became the founding tape of the entire movement. "If the frost takes the grain, we will stitch the sky with iron," shouted Amrit into the pitch-black corridor as the sax reed cracked under his fingers.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Stark album art showing a rusted iron vault door cracking open over a field of glowing, frozen wheat seeds, with raw yarn threads pulling the ice back together under a dark, bruised sky.

Track 2: Loom-Stitched Boli No. 9
- Story & Context: Deep in the decommissioned Coal Mine #3, lead singer Gurpreet sat alone with a broken tumbi, writing a confession about the family farm she abandoned in Punjab to work the Arctic rigs. She warped the traditional celebration song into an angry, distorted scream, backed only by a looping baritone sax feedback loop that sounded like a dying freight train. When the rest of the Cartel snuck into the shaft with their amps, the track exploded into a violent, sludgy hard-rock breakdown that caused a minor cave-in. "I traded warm soil for cold iron, but my tongue still burns like mustard oil," she spat into a dynamic mic wrapped in barbed wire.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Surreal imagery of an ancient wooden loom weaving heavy, glowing copper cables into a traditional Punjabi shawl, set inside a pitch-black, frost-covered mine shaft illuminated by amber safety flares.

Track 3: Who Dropped the Thread in the Silo?
- Story & Context: Years after the seed vault incident, local authorities finally attempted to breach the subterranean rave space during a sub-zero blizzard. The Svalbard Boli Cartel did not flee; instead, they looped the sound of an industrial grain auger through four baritone sax amplifiers turned up to maximum volume. As the tear gas hissed through the vents, eight hundred fans sang the track's final, crushing vocal hook in unison, their boots shaking snow from the ceiling until the police retreated. "Leave the vault unlocked or watch this whole mountain unravel," echoes the final boot-stomp fadeout of the EP.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: High-contrast concert poster aesthetic showing a dense crowd of arctic-clad rebels with glowing blue eyes holding broken saxophones aloft inside a massive, cylindrical grain silo filled with swirling snow and gold thread.

